WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.842
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:07.112 --> 00:09.114
[theme music playing]

3
00:30.261 --> 00:33.681
[bell chiming]

4
00:41.814 --> 00:43.023
[dramatic music playing]

5
00:43.107 --> 00:45.442
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

6
00:46.443 --> 00:47.444
That man…

7
00:55.202 --> 00:59.665
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

8
01:01.250 --> 01:03.460
Kang San is indeed my father.

9
01:04.169 --> 01:07.131
So that man is your father, isn't he, Chris?

10
01:07.965 --> 01:09.133
Then please…

11
01:11.135 --> 01:15.764
tell us what is your Korean name, Chris?

12
01:20.644 --> 01:21.687
[grunts]

13
01:27.985 --> 01:29.695
Chris.

14
01:29.778 --> 01:34.450
Please tell us, what is your Korean name, Chris?

15
01:50.132 --> 01:52.217
[breathing rapidly]

16
02:02.811 --> 02:04.605
[breathing deeply]

17
02:26.877 --> 02:28.462
-[panting] -[door closes]

18
02:34.009 --> 02:35.427
My Korean name is…

19
02:38.389 --> 02:39.598
is Kang Han.

20
02:41.642 --> 02:43.435
[dramatic music continues playing]

21
02:53.779 --> 02:54.863
What is all this?

22
02:57.157 --> 02:59.535
Who told you you had any authority to go off script?

23
02:59.618 --> 03:02.079
And where did you get all those photos of him from?

24
03:03.998 --> 03:05.040
Or…

25
03:09.128 --> 03:10.462
CAPTAIN COOK

26
03:11.046 --> 03:12.715
Was there a different script from the start?

27
03:14.049 --> 03:15.718
Uh, well…

28
03:16.510 --> 03:18.595
What comes next, huh?

29
03:18.679 --> 03:20.848
What other surprises do you have in store, huh?

30
03:21.515 --> 03:24.101
Um, excuse me, where should I cut to?

31
03:24.184 --> 03:25.728
We've been holding this shot too long.

32
03:25.811 --> 03:28.731
[Sang-hyeon] Uh, go wide on the stage for now.

33
03:31.567 --> 03:33.694
Uh, Captain Cook. Move on to the next question.

34
03:33.777 --> 03:35.529
The one about Chris K.'s childhood…

35
03:37.614 --> 03:38.615
[sighs]

36
03:39.658 --> 03:42.286
-Get up now. -What?

37
03:42.369 --> 03:44.079
I said get out of that chair.

38
03:44.955 --> 03:47.166
[laughing] Ah, geez…

39
03:47.249 --> 03:49.168
I'm telling you to get out of my seat now!

40
03:52.504 --> 03:54.006
[intense music playing]

41
04:22.409 --> 04:25.287
[laughing]

42
04:25.370 --> 04:29.500
Well, the message board is blowing up with excited and anxious viewers

43
04:29.583 --> 04:32.377
demanding that we announce the next challenge already.

44
04:32.461 --> 04:35.589
Being able to interact with viewers and incorporate their feedback in real time

45
04:35.672 --> 04:37.758
is part of the fun of live television, isn't it?

46
04:37.841 --> 04:41.553
You know our number, so call in and participate.

47
04:42.221 --> 04:44.973
Now, to give the chefs as much cooking time as possible,

48
04:45.057 --> 04:47.851
we'll wrap up our little game of guess who's who here.

49
04:47.935 --> 04:51.313
Stay tuned because we might reveal a little more about our chefs later on.

50
04:51.396 --> 04:52.898
And now, attention!

51
04:53.482 --> 04:58.070
I will announce the challenge for this round.

52
05:29.309 --> 05:30.602
[audience exclaiming]

53
05:30.686 --> 05:32.396
CHEONGJU

54
05:47.995 --> 05:50.414
Stand by for entry. Go.

55
06:15.439 --> 06:17.441
Chefs, please head to your labs.

56
06:48.513 --> 06:50.641
JEHYANGJU

57
07:15.874 --> 07:18.043
Ready!

58
07:21.630 --> 07:23.006
[shouts]

59
07:35.769 --> 07:36.895
[drummer grunts]

60
07:50.867 --> 07:52.661
[dramatic music playing]

61
07:52.744 --> 07:56.957
[Captain Cook] A liquor where subtraction matters more than addition.

62
08:00.419 --> 08:02.129
A gift that can only be earned

63
08:02.212 --> 08:06.008
after enduring the long wait through a hundred days of aging.

64
08:12.139 --> 08:14.016
Our traditional Korean liquor,

65
08:14.099 --> 08:18.353
lost when the tradition of home brewing was broken during Japanese colonial rule.

66
08:20.939 --> 08:23.150
Made with our rice, our water

67
08:23.233 --> 08:25.777
and our traditional fermentation starter, nuruk.

68
08:27.612 --> 08:32.617
And yet, our own liquor cannot be called by its own name. Cheongju.

69
08:48.175 --> 08:50.010
[drumming continues]

70
09:09.071 --> 09:11.323
[audience applauding, cheering]

71
09:30.634 --> 09:32.386
CHEONGJU

72
09:35.806 --> 09:39.184
Now, we use Jehyangju as traditional liquor.

73
09:39.810 --> 09:44.189
First brewed and developed here in Haeon, but will soon fade into obscurity.

74
09:45.273 --> 09:50.487
In order to help our traditional liquor reclaim its name, cheongju.

75
10:02.958 --> 10:04.084
What are you up to?

76
10:06.753 --> 10:08.839
[breathing shakily]

77
10:08.922 --> 10:10.006
Chun-ja, you…

78
10:15.971 --> 10:18.432
I don't think you're in any position to be here right now.

79
10:22.811 --> 10:23.979
What do you mean?

80
10:24.813 --> 10:26.773
Explain it so I can understand.

81
10:28.066 --> 10:29.151
Answer me!

82
10:33.196 --> 10:35.115
You just saw it for yourself.

83
10:36.783 --> 10:40.954
If you still don't understand what's going on after seeing your son…

84
10:43.373 --> 10:46.042
I don't know what else I can say to help you understand.

85
10:46.126 --> 10:47.919
[sighs]

86
10:51.047 --> 10:52.048
Huh?

87
10:53.300 --> 10:54.718
Chairwoman?

88
11:05.479 --> 11:06.897
Chris!

89
11:07.731 --> 11:09.733
-Chris. -Chris. What's wrong?

90
11:09.816 --> 11:11.359
-Chris. -Chris, wake up.

91
11:11.943 --> 11:13.778
Michael, go get the production crew. Hurry!

92
11:13.862 --> 11:16.490
-Yes, Chef. -Chris. Get him on his back.

93
11:16.573 --> 11:17.657
Chris, are you okay?

94
11:17.741 --> 11:20.160
Chris, come on, wake up. Hey, Chris!

95
11:21.328 --> 11:22.662
What's up?

96
11:23.205 --> 11:24.956
It looks like Chris K. collapsed.

97
11:25.040 --> 11:26.500
I'll cut to Lab 1.

98
11:38.178 --> 11:39.387
Damn it.

99
11:39.471 --> 11:42.140
Ms. Seon. The live chat is blowing up right now.

100
11:42.224 --> 11:44.142
[viewer] No way. Did Chris K. just collapse?

101
11:44.226 --> 11:46.895
-What happened? Does anyone know? -[viewer 3] Looks like something happened.

102
11:46.978 --> 11:49.397
[viewer 4] I guess anything can happen on live TV.

103
11:51.107 --> 11:52.651
Cut to the outdoor stage now.

104
11:52.734 --> 11:54.444
How much nanta drumming do we still have?

105
11:54.528 --> 11:55.570
About 30 seconds.

106
11:57.239 --> 11:58.448
Thirty seconds?

107
11:58.532 --> 12:01.910
Captain Cook, once the performance ends, say something to the audience.

108
12:01.993 --> 12:05.372
Just anything. We'll bring Mr. Park Seon-woong onstage early.

109
12:05.455 --> 12:07.499
Jehyangju. Talk about Jehyangju.

110
12:08.333 --> 12:11.044
[sighs] Just stall for a bit. We've got a situation over here.

111
12:11.962 --> 12:14.130
Don't show the kitchen feed on the outdoor screens yet.

112
12:14.214 --> 12:15.757
Okay.

113
12:15.840 --> 12:19.761
Hey, Yoon. We need your help. Get Mr. Park Seon-woong on now!

114
12:25.183 --> 12:26.184
Excuse me, sir.

115
12:27.477 --> 12:29.479
There's something I need to tell you.

116
12:31.690 --> 12:33.275
How long have you known?

117
12:35.902 --> 12:37.362
How long have you known?

118
12:38.071 --> 12:40.323
I don't think that's important at all right now.

119
12:40.907 --> 12:42.242
What'd you say?

120
12:42.325 --> 12:44.369
Tomorrow morning… No.

121
12:44.869 --> 12:46.997
The second this broadcast ends tonight,

122
12:47.080 --> 12:51.251
a swarm of reporters will come at you from every direction, hungry for answers.

123
12:51.334 --> 12:53.211
"So you actually have a son?"

124
12:53.295 --> 12:55.171
"Didn't you say he died years ago?"

125
12:55.255 --> 12:56.673
"Is he really your son?"

126
12:56.756 --> 12:58.925
"What happened to your husband?"

127
12:59.009 --> 13:01.136
"Why is your son only showing up now?"

128
13:01.219 --> 13:04.723
"Were your son and his father living together or separately?"

129
13:04.806 --> 13:06.808
"Why did you and your husband split up?"

130
13:06.891 --> 13:08.268
[inhales sharply] Before you know it,

131
13:08.351 --> 13:10.729
someone will have already dug into our entire family history,

132
13:10.812 --> 13:13.857
put together a neat little family tree and posted it online.

133
13:13.940 --> 13:16.151
And someone else might suddenly come up with

134
13:16.234 --> 13:18.528
a very interesting take on this competition.

135
13:19.279 --> 13:20.447
"What's this now?

136
13:21.197 --> 13:24.159
Well… does this mean she spent all that money

137
13:24.242 --> 13:25.952
staging this huge competition,

138
13:26.036 --> 13:27.495
going on about the future of K-food,

139
13:27.579 --> 13:30.540
just to make sure that the son she'd kept hidden would win?"

140
13:30.624 --> 13:32.667
"She said she was scrapping Plate in Asia

141
13:32.751 --> 13:35.587
to focus on getting back to the company's roots as a food business,

142
13:35.670 --> 13:38.173
but… [gasps] No way!

143
13:38.256 --> 13:41.426
Was all of that just bullshit?"

144
13:43.887 --> 13:46.806
[gasping breaths]

145
13:47.390 --> 13:50.518
[groaning]

146
13:51.728 --> 13:53.438
Oh, what am I going to do?

147
13:56.191 --> 13:59.277
[whimpering] Oh, what do I do? What should I do now?

148
13:59.361 --> 14:01.446
[crying]

149
14:01.529 --> 14:02.530
What?

150
14:02.614 --> 14:04.407
What should you do now?

151
14:05.575 --> 14:08.244
Are you asking me right now, huh?

152
14:09.579 --> 14:11.373
What… [laughing]

153
14:17.837 --> 14:19.631
[laughing hysterically]

154
14:27.597 --> 14:29.599
[screaming]

155
14:36.856 --> 14:38.358
What shall I do for you?

156
14:39.150 --> 14:41.277
What do you want me to do?

157
14:41.903 --> 14:44.906
What? Should I spill all those old stories?

158
14:46.366 --> 14:48.368
The one about how you framed your husband,

159
14:48.451 --> 14:49.452
sent him to prison

160
14:49.536 --> 14:52.080
and made him sign away his parental rights.

161
14:53.248 --> 14:56.084
Or the one about how your son escaped to see his dad,

162
14:56.167 --> 14:59.003
only for you to drag him back, lock him up and abuse him?

163
14:59.087 --> 15:00.171
[gasps]

164
15:00.255 --> 15:02.966
That's better than any soap opera, don't you think?

165
15:03.049 --> 15:05.552
No, wait. If we're going to do this…

166
15:05.635 --> 15:08.680
Yes, if we're going to expose it all, we might as well go big.

167
15:08.763 --> 15:10.432
Yes, really, really big!

168
15:10.515 --> 15:11.766
How about the one about a mother

169
15:11.850 --> 15:14.102
who chased her husband all the way to America,

170
15:14.185 --> 15:15.729
tormented him,

171
15:15.812 --> 15:19.190
eventually killed him and abandoned her own son?

172
15:19.274 --> 15:20.400
That's right.

173
15:20.984 --> 15:22.485
A story this good,

174
15:22.569 --> 15:26.030
this spectacular can't be kept under wraps, don't you agree?

175
15:26.114 --> 15:29.117
What a story! [laughing]

176
15:34.247 --> 15:35.457
What are…

177
15:36.374 --> 15:37.709
What are you talking about?

178
15:38.293 --> 15:40.462
Who killed whom? Why would I wanna kill him?

179
15:40.545 --> 15:43.089
[shouting] What on earth are you talking about?

180
15:43.173 --> 15:45.133
[laughing]

181
15:52.891 --> 15:54.058
You don't remember?

182
15:55.101 --> 15:59.647
You said it yourself that day when you made me kneel down.

183
16:14.579 --> 16:16.956
"Sometimes, you need to burn the whole house down…

184
16:18.792 --> 16:22.462
[both] …just to get rid of a single bedbug that won't let you sleep."

185
16:31.846 --> 16:33.598
You said it didn't matter if he died.

186
16:37.602 --> 16:39.979
That's what you said, remember?

187
16:45.610 --> 16:47.862
You said there was no problem if he died,

188
16:47.946 --> 16:49.656
as long as you got your son back.

189
16:49.739 --> 16:50.907
[gasps]

190
16:50.990 --> 16:51.991
You…

191
16:52.075 --> 16:55.328
Yes. You said so yourself. You did.

192
16:56.663 --> 16:57.789
You did!

193
16:58.957 --> 17:00.875
You did!

194
17:05.255 --> 17:06.965
[clattering]

195
17:08.925 --> 17:10.760
"Hello, sir. Good evening.

196
17:10.844 --> 17:12.136
Good evening.

197
17:12.220 --> 17:13.221
Good evening."

198
17:14.639 --> 17:17.308
Um, "First of all, what was the very first thing that came to mind?"

199
17:18.184 --> 17:19.185
[sighs, clicks tongue]

200
17:19.269 --> 17:22.480
Uh, making nuruk… It means…

201
17:22.564 --> 17:26.860
Let's put the kitchen feed on the stage screens now.

202
17:26.943 --> 17:30.113
But keep Lab 2 off for now. Only show Labs 1 and 3.

203
17:30.196 --> 17:31.197
Okay.

204
17:33.491 --> 17:35.577
Hey, Yoon, come to the truck right now.

205
17:47.547 --> 17:50.425
-Should we add more mayo to the gochujang? -Yes, a little more.

206
17:50.508 --> 17:52.176
THIS IS NOT GIMBAP

207
17:57.599 --> 17:59.475
Let's switch to Lab 3.

208
18:26.711 --> 18:28.880
-Damn it, what is this? -[Ja-ryong] Hey!

209
18:28.963 --> 18:31.925
-[gasps] -Oh, no.

210
18:42.560 --> 18:44.646
I said don't show Lab 2 yet!

211
18:46.022 --> 18:48.608
Hey, I don't get it. Why won't they show our team?

212
18:49.192 --> 18:50.526
Did I miss them or something?

213
18:50.610 --> 18:51.736
I'm not sure.

214
18:52.737 --> 18:54.030
What the hell is going on here?

215
18:55.406 --> 18:56.741
Man, I don't like this.

216
18:56.824 --> 19:00.078
Do you think that something might've gone wrong?

217
19:02.705 --> 19:04.999
[audience member] I'm in the audience watching the stage screen.

218
19:05.083 --> 19:07.001
Looks like something happened in Kitchen 3.

219
19:07.085 --> 19:10.213
[viewer] Why do I only see Team Exception in Lab 2? I don't see Chris K.

220
19:10.296 --> 19:12.757
[viewer 2] I thought I saw Chef Chris collapse in the kitchen earlier.

221
19:12.840 --> 19:14.467
-Was it just me? -[viewer 3] If there was an accident,

222
19:14.550 --> 19:17.387
shouldn't they at least put up a message on screen, tell us what happened?

223
19:17.470 --> 19:19.639
[viewer 4] Did something really happen to Chris K. earlier today?

224
19:19.722 --> 19:21.516
Ms. Seon, the chat again.

225
19:25.645 --> 19:27.438
Cam 3, come on. Are you still not ready?

226
19:28.314 --> 19:29.315
Ms. Seon!

227
19:43.788 --> 19:45.164
Why are you here already?

228
19:45.248 --> 19:47.041
The judge interview is still a long way off.

229
19:47.125 --> 19:49.377
Don't ask. They just told us to start interviewing anyone.

230
19:49.460 --> 19:50.586
What? Who did?

231
19:50.670 --> 19:53.214
Who else? The chief producer. Let us through.

232
19:55.341 --> 19:56.634
Hi.

233
20:18.781 --> 20:20.074
Stop looking over there.

234
20:20.992 --> 20:23.995
If they're not back in 20 minutes, we go with Plan B.

235
20:24.579 --> 20:28.082
Mix the ssamjang and Jehyangju, then marinate the pork jowl in it.

236
20:28.791 --> 20:30.918
It needs to marinate for at least an hour.

237
20:31.502 --> 20:32.920
Yes, Chef.

238
20:38.342 --> 20:40.386
[gasping]

239
20:42.055 --> 20:43.056
[inhales shakily]

240
20:43.139 --> 20:44.307
Chris.

241
20:46.184 --> 20:48.102
[door opens, closes]

242
20:49.062 --> 20:51.773
Mr. Kang. I see you're coming around.

243
21:00.865 --> 21:02.325
[panting] Chris.

244
21:08.247 --> 21:11.084
[doctor] Although he wasn't out for an unreasonable amount of time,

245
21:11.167 --> 21:14.087
given that he lost consciousness and his sense of balance hasn't returned,

246
21:14.170 --> 21:16.714
his sense of taste and smell may be a little impaired for some time.

247
21:16.798 --> 21:17.799
That means…

248
21:17.882 --> 21:21.385
It's really hard to say for sure until we've run some more tests on him.

249
21:22.053 --> 21:25.890
He may also experience seizures or paralysis.

250
21:26.516 --> 21:29.060
Its best to take him to a larger hospital right away.

251
21:29.143 --> 21:32.396
And it would be dangerous for him to compete any further.

252
21:34.649 --> 21:36.859
Well, I'll have an ambulance ready.

253
21:38.986 --> 21:39.987
No.

254
21:42.824 --> 21:44.826
I'm staying in the competition.

255
21:45.409 --> 21:46.410
Chris.

256
21:56.212 --> 22:00.091
Every dish we prepared will be served as planned.

257
22:02.844 --> 22:04.512
Don't worry about it.

258
22:04.595 --> 22:06.139
I will not let you down.

259
22:06.222 --> 22:08.975
I'm sorry, but this isn't something you can push through on willpower alone.

260
22:09.058 --> 22:11.894
She's right. You still need to take some time to rest.

261
22:12.478 --> 22:15.439
-After you've had a few more tests-- -Relax, I won't be doing the cooking.

262
22:16.190 --> 22:17.859
Team Famiglia's dishes served today…

263
22:19.944 --> 22:23.156
will be prepared by Chef Choi Song-yi.

264
22:51.267 --> 22:52.685
[sighs]

265
22:57.440 --> 22:58.649
[breathing shakily]

266
23:00.067 --> 23:02.028
[door opens]

267
23:03.446 --> 23:05.364
[gasping]

268
23:19.128 --> 23:21.756
[gentle music playing]

269
24:10.930 --> 24:12.890
[guest judge] Being back here makes me wonder

270
24:12.974 --> 24:15.059
where the past 40 years have gone to.

271
24:15.726 --> 24:16.811
When I went to school here,

272
24:16.894 --> 24:19.313
we used to share our desk with another student.

273
24:19.397 --> 24:22.108
We'd draw a line down the middle and warn our desk mate

274
24:22.191 --> 24:24.443
not to cross to our side or else.

275
24:24.527 --> 24:25.778
[laughing]

276
24:25.861 --> 24:27.905
If someone brought something tasty for lunch,

277
24:27.989 --> 24:31.075
we'd end up getting into fights to try and steal some.

278
24:31.158 --> 24:35.246
Right, we all get worked up over the smallest things at that age, don't we?

279
24:35.329 --> 24:38.165
It sounds like all those memories are coming back to you now.

280
24:38.249 --> 24:39.959
-Yeah. -[chuckling]

281
24:41.544 --> 24:43.754
[mouthing] Keep going. Come on.

282
24:43.838 --> 24:45.881
[mouthing] What more am I supposed to do?

283
24:53.472 --> 24:55.474
Next, we'll speak with Chairwoman Moon Hae-seon

284
24:55.558 --> 24:57.059
of Pyeongyeon Group.

285
24:58.644 --> 25:00.604
Hello, ma'am. How are you?

286
25:00.688 --> 25:05.318
Well, I was surprised to find out you attended Haeon Elementary School as a kid.

287
25:05.401 --> 25:08.571
Tell us, madam, is there a particular chef you're rooting for tonight?

288
25:11.657 --> 25:13.159
Um, or since today's challenge

289
25:13.242 --> 25:16.245
is all about the nostalgic street food Koreans eat every day,

290
25:16.329 --> 25:18.456
is there anything in particular you would like to see

291
25:18.539 --> 25:20.333
our talented competitors cooking today?

292
25:23.878 --> 25:27.423
Um… Maybe a personal favorite, or something you'd recommend,

293
25:27.506 --> 25:29.050
something from your childhood…

294
25:30.718 --> 25:33.054
Well, it could be anything, really…

295
25:33.137 --> 25:37.600
[muffled] For example, something you ate a long time ago that's stayed with you.

296
25:37.683 --> 25:40.770
Anything at all, like playground food or…

297
25:44.482 --> 25:45.691
[groans]

298
25:47.193 --> 25:48.527
[reporter, muffled] Ma'am?

299
25:51.322 --> 25:52.573
Are you okay?

300
25:54.825 --> 25:56.035
What's wrong?

301
26:00.414 --> 26:03.793
[Hyeon-ju cackling]

302
26:12.676 --> 26:15.679
I'm so excited, I could just dance. [cackles]

303
26:17.890 --> 26:20.476
She can struggle all she wants.

304
26:21.852 --> 26:26.023
There's no way out for her now. [laughing]

305
26:29.860 --> 26:31.904
The way her lips were trembling like…

306
26:32.446 --> 26:34.949
Oh, Hyeong-koo, you really should've seen her.

307
26:35.574 --> 26:36.742
My goodness.

308
26:37.451 --> 26:40.955
Who'd have thought that steely woman would crumble like that?

309
26:41.038 --> 26:45.292
I'm telling you, children, they'll be the death of you. Really.

310
26:46.752 --> 26:49.839
Once the broadcast ends, calls are going to start pouring in.

311
26:49.922 --> 26:53.175
Now that everyone knows he's the chairwoman's son,

312
26:53.259 --> 26:56.345
reporters will be swarming all of us tomorrow morning.

313
26:57.847 --> 26:59.306
You.

314
26:59.390 --> 27:02.351
Whatever you do, don't make any official statements.

315
27:02.435 --> 27:04.770
Just say we're still verifying the facts, got it?

316
27:04.854 --> 27:07.022
Yes, Ms. Baek. Yes, of course.

317
27:07.106 --> 27:08.149
-Koo. -Yes?

318
27:09.108 --> 27:11.944
-There is an order to these things. -Ah.

319
27:12.027 --> 27:13.487
-Look. -Oh, yes.

320
27:13.571 --> 27:18.284
First, you get some professionals to spread it all over social media.

321
27:19.076 --> 27:20.369
-Spread it. -"Spread it."

322
27:20.453 --> 27:23.956
Then you feed it to channels that know how to turn gossip like this

323
27:24.039 --> 27:25.332
into good content.

324
27:25.833 --> 27:27.251
-Feed it. -"Feed it."

325
27:27.334 --> 27:31.088
You see, nowadays, that's where reporters get all their stories.

326
27:31.172 --> 27:34.884
-Oh. -Sauce. Yeah, like donkasseu sauce.

327
27:34.967 --> 27:37.678
A little at a time. Don't give them everything at once.

328
27:37.761 --> 27:43.517
Like this. Drip… drip… drip… drip… drip… drip.

329
27:43.601 --> 27:45.603
Just a little taste at a time. Like this.

330
27:45.686 --> 27:46.896
[clicking tongue]

331
27:46.979 --> 27:47.980
A story arc?

332
27:48.063 --> 27:51.066
Yeah, yes, bit by bit. Well, you know what I mean!

333
27:51.150 --> 27:54.570
Like those killer cliff-hangers that leave you dying to see the next episode.

334
27:54.653 --> 27:55.738
You know what I mean, right?

335
27:55.821 --> 27:57.823
-Tell me you know what I mean! -Yes, yes.

336
27:57.907 --> 27:58.908
-Okay? -Okay.

337
27:58.991 --> 28:00.951
I-I know what you mean. I understand.

338
28:07.082 --> 28:08.626
Tasty memories?

339
28:08.709 --> 28:10.127
[reporter] "Tasty memories?"

340
28:10.211 --> 28:12.338
[judge] I can't think of a better answer for today's challenge.

341
28:12.421 --> 28:15.132
Ms. Yoon, we're getting a ton of complaints that it's boring

342
28:15.216 --> 28:17.343
because we've been showing nothing but interviews.

343
28:17.426 --> 28:18.969
They're saying they'll switch channels.

344
28:19.803 --> 28:22.223
They're done with another interview. They can't keep this up.

345
28:22.306 --> 28:24.266
Let's just cut back to the cooking.

346
28:24.350 --> 28:25.726
[sighs]

347
28:26.727 --> 28:29.063
Can I really switch feeds without Ms. Seon's okay?

348
28:31.482 --> 28:33.651
-Okay. Let's cut to the audience for now. -Okay.

349
28:33.734 --> 28:36.153
Stand by on the main judges.

350
28:36.737 --> 28:37.738
Hey, Kim.

351
28:38.364 --> 28:39.573
I can't.

352
28:39.657 --> 28:40.950
I can't do this, Chris.

353
28:41.033 --> 28:43.410
You can do it. You have to.

354
28:43.494 --> 28:45.412
Chris. This is impossible.

355
28:45.496 --> 28:47.498
How am I supposed to cook something this important right now?

356
28:47.581 --> 28:50.376
You can do it. Just do what we practiced this morning--

357
28:50.459 --> 28:51.710
I said I can't!

358
28:51.794 --> 28:53.045
I can't do it, Chris.

359
28:53.128 --> 28:55.047
-Song. -No, Chris, please…

360
29:06.642 --> 29:07.851
Look at me.

361
29:08.477 --> 29:09.979
Look me in the eye.

362
29:10.646 --> 29:11.647
Song!

363
29:18.529 --> 29:20.281
It's all my fault.

364
29:20.990 --> 29:23.033
It's all because of that stupid curse.

365
29:23.659 --> 29:26.996
I should've been more careful. I should've taken more precautions.

366
29:27.079 --> 29:29.832
If I had, none of this would have happened to you.

367
29:30.457 --> 29:32.334
It's all my fault.

368
29:32.418 --> 29:35.170
It's all because of my stubbornness and my stupid curse.

369
29:38.966 --> 29:40.968
Why did you choose me if you know about my curse?

370
29:41.051 --> 29:43.178
You should've chosen someone with experience.

371
29:43.262 --> 29:46.599
Why pick a complete beginner like me with no cooking experience?

372
29:46.682 --> 29:49.143
Look at me. I can't do anything, can't you see?

373
29:49.768 --> 29:52.354
Even in a crisis like this, all I can do is cry.

374
29:52.438 --> 29:54.648
So why did you choose me? Why did you choose me?

375
29:55.232 --> 29:58.152
Why did you choose someone as useless as me? Why?

376
29:58.235 --> 29:59.820
Why?

377
29:59.903 --> 30:01.405
[crying]

378
30:04.825 --> 30:06.493
Because the owner of Famiglia

379
30:07.870 --> 30:08.954
is you, Song.

380
30:24.845 --> 30:26.388
The owner of Famiglia

381
30:27.264 --> 30:29.516
is not anyone else.

382
30:30.184 --> 30:31.852
It's you, Choi Song-yi.

383
30:33.562 --> 30:35.689
Once this is over and I leave,

384
30:36.982 --> 30:38.692
who's going to lead Famiglia?

385
30:40.319 --> 30:41.737
Du-yeong?

386
30:41.820 --> 30:43.238
Geon-woo?

387
30:43.989 --> 30:44.990
No.

388
30:47.242 --> 30:48.994
The person who should lead Famiglia

389
30:50.621 --> 30:52.164
is you, Choi Song-yi.

390
30:56.585 --> 30:58.087
Have you forgotten?

391
31:00.506 --> 31:01.674
Your father.

392
31:04.843 --> 31:06.637
He was killed by Pyeongyeon.

393
31:08.347 --> 31:09.848
We have to beat them.

394
31:11.183 --> 31:12.267
Together.

395
31:14.645 --> 31:19.942
[inhales sharply] This dish… No, this round began…

396
31:23.570 --> 31:25.489
the moment you picked up on the scent

397
31:26.949 --> 31:28.951
of lotus flowers and found Jehyangju.

398
31:30.786 --> 31:33.539
Without you, this challenge would've been impossible from the start.

399
31:37.501 --> 31:38.502
Song.

400
31:39.169 --> 31:40.170
Listen, Song.

401
31:40.879 --> 31:44.883
You have instincts and sensitivity that no one else has.

402
31:44.967 --> 31:46.677
I know you do.

403
31:46.760 --> 31:47.886
I know…

404
31:50.305 --> 31:52.015
you'll do this better than anyone.

405
31:59.523 --> 32:00.649
Remember this.

406
32:00.733 --> 32:04.069
Cooking isn't about technique.

407
32:05.821 --> 32:07.573
It's about memory and imagination.

408
32:11.410 --> 32:12.911
Song. Listen.

409
32:17.291 --> 32:19.585
Remember the pitch and volume of this sound.

410
32:20.878 --> 32:22.379
Don't rely only on your sight alone.

411
32:22.463 --> 32:24.423
Sometimes, sound tells you more.

412
32:24.506 --> 32:27.468
And don't rely too much on the thermometer.

413
32:27.551 --> 32:28.635
It could ruin this dish.

414
32:30.429 --> 32:31.430
Okay.

415
32:33.098 --> 32:34.099
Song.

416
32:34.725 --> 32:37.853
Smell and taste can actually cloud your judgment.

417
32:38.479 --> 32:41.148
What matters more than focusing on all senses

418
32:41.648 --> 32:43.609
is the picture a chef has

419
32:44.318 --> 32:45.819
in their mind.

420
32:45.903 --> 32:49.281
It's not just about taste. It's what you are trying to express.

421
32:49.364 --> 32:50.616
That's the key.

422
32:54.453 --> 32:57.247
[sighs] Remember just two things in this round.

423
32:57.790 --> 32:59.583
Memory and imagination.

424
33:20.103 --> 33:21.730
ONGOJISIM

425
33:34.409 --> 33:38.539
Tell us a little about your dish. It looks like you're prepping sea anemone.

426
33:38.622 --> 33:40.457
Yes. This is swimming anemone.

427
33:40.541 --> 33:42.000
It's an unusual species

428
33:42.084 --> 33:44.920
found only in much deeper waters than ordinary sea anemones.

429
33:45.003 --> 33:46.505
It has a distinctive shape

430
33:46.588 --> 33:48.715
and a chewier texture than other sea anemones,

431
33:48.799 --> 33:51.260
so it's sometimes served boiled.

432
33:51.343 --> 33:52.970
Oh. [chuckles]

433
33:53.053 --> 33:57.474
I had no idea swimming anemones were found off the coastal waters of Haeon.

434
33:57.558 --> 34:00.936
Uh, so what are you planning to make with it? What's the dish called?

435
34:02.604 --> 34:07.401
Well, it looks like you're lining a square mold with noodles over there.

436
34:07.484 --> 34:09.945
[chuckling] It actually reminds me of lasagna.

437
34:10.028 --> 34:11.697
Well, I'm preparing a noodle bowl.

438
34:11.780 --> 34:13.031
A noodle bowl?

439
34:13.115 --> 34:15.617
I'm using the noodles themselves to make the bowl.

440
34:15.701 --> 34:18.328
Oh, so you're making a bowl out of noodles.

441
34:19.037 --> 34:21.415
Wouldn't the broth leak through?

442
34:21.498 --> 34:23.208
It might be difficult to eat out of.

443
34:23.792 --> 34:25.043
It would, right?

444
34:25.711 --> 34:28.338
So obviously, we came up with a way around that.

445
34:33.427 --> 34:34.553
[chuckles]

446
34:50.527 --> 34:52.696
IT CAN'T HAVE BEEN EASY TO GIVE A TWIST

447
34:52.779 --> 34:54.448
TO A FAMILIAR FOOD LIKE GIMBAP

448
34:54.531 --> 34:57.159
We thought gimbap was an essential part of Korean street food.

449
34:57.242 --> 34:58.535
ISABEL DOMINGUEZ HEAD CHEF OF INTEGRAL

450
34:58.619 --> 35:00.078
JOSHUA YEONG-HO PAYTON OWNER-CHEF OF HUSH

451
35:00.162 --> 35:02.581
But we wanted to take something as familiar and ordinary as gimbap

452
35:02.664 --> 35:04.875
and give it a glamorous and unusual twist, you see.

453
35:04.958 --> 35:09.212
For tradition to continue, it must be accompanied by innovation.

454
35:09.296 --> 35:11.757
So we decided, "Why not turn it inside out?"

455
35:12.424 --> 35:16.303
When I was in school, the cafeteria used to serve celery sticks.

456
35:16.386 --> 35:18.889
But the kids would never eat them like that.

457
35:18.972 --> 35:21.558
They'd spread a thick layer of peanut butter on them.

458
35:22.225 --> 35:25.479
So, the peanut butter's nutty flavor got the kids eating them just fine.

459
35:25.562 --> 35:29.483
Of course, some kids would just scoop off the peanut butter with their fingers

460
35:29.566 --> 35:31.401
-and throw the celery sticks away. -[Isabel laughing]

461
35:31.485 --> 35:33.487
Which got us thinking

462
35:33.570 --> 35:36.865
about another healthy vegetable kids tend not to eat.

463
35:36.949 --> 35:39.701
We thought of carrots. That's a core ingredient of gimbap.

464
35:39.785 --> 35:42.829
So our idea was to slice the carrots thin, blanch them

465
35:42.913 --> 35:44.706
and use them to wrap the rice and fillings.

466
35:45.290 --> 35:48.377
[Isabel] Then cut the seaweed into thin strips and wrap them around the carrot.

467
35:48.460 --> 35:51.672
Oh, and of course, we used a very special kind of dried seaweed,

468
35:51.755 --> 35:53.131
brushed with hawthorn berry oil.

469
35:53.215 --> 35:56.259
Carrots usually go on the inside and dried seaweed on the outside.

470
35:56.343 --> 35:58.178
Here, they wrap around each other,

471
35:58.261 --> 36:01.056
so there's no clear distinction between inside and outside.

472
36:01.139 --> 36:03.600
So it's not your traditional gimbap.

473
36:03.684 --> 36:04.726
And for its name,

474
36:04.810 --> 36:09.773
I took a little inspiration from René Magritte's painting This Is Not A Pipe.

475
36:12.985 --> 36:15.278
That's why "This Is Not Gimbap."

476
36:16.154 --> 36:18.573
THIS IS NOT GIMBAP

477
36:20.909 --> 36:22.619
No good.

478
36:23.620 --> 36:25.831
I can't get any of the cheongju aroma at all.

479
36:30.544 --> 36:32.379
-[groans] -[utensil clatters]

480
36:32.462 --> 36:33.463
You're right.

481
36:34.423 --> 36:38.010
See, even though the sugar, white doenjang and tomato sauce

482
36:38.093 --> 36:39.594
have toned down the heat,

483
36:39.678 --> 36:42.055
the gochujang itself has too much body to begin with.

484
36:42.973 --> 36:46.351
Maybe with distilled liquor, but it really clashes with cheongju.

485
36:46.435 --> 36:47.561
Right?

486
36:47.644 --> 36:49.646
It's driving me crazy. [groans]

487
36:50.147 --> 36:52.482
Should we have gone with something else from the start?

488
36:54.317 --> 36:55.318
[sous-chef] Chef.

489
36:55.402 --> 36:56.903
Could you take a look at this?

490
36:57.571 --> 36:59.865
It's way too soft to use as a sundae casing.

491
37:02.367 --> 37:04.286
Man, what should we do?

492
37:04.369 --> 37:07.122
Should we scrap the vegetable tangsu with the tteokbokki sauce

493
37:07.205 --> 37:09.541
and make… something else?

494
37:11.793 --> 37:13.587
[sighs] I'm not sure.

495
37:13.670 --> 37:17.340
[inhales sharply] We're not gonna suddenly come up with some brilliant new idea.

496
37:17.424 --> 37:19.092
How about a soy-based sauce?

497
37:19.176 --> 37:21.928
Then we'd have to go with meat instead of vegetables.

498
37:22.012 --> 37:24.973
-That's just too ordinary. -[sighs]

499
37:25.849 --> 37:28.685
Ah, geez. I should've just believed him.

500
37:30.020 --> 37:32.439
I mean, I thought he was bluffing, so I thought I would…

501
37:32.522 --> 37:34.775
-Well, damn it. -What?

502
37:35.358 --> 37:38.195
How was I supposed to know it would really be cheongju?

503
37:38.862 --> 37:42.407
I never thought for a second that he'd share information that important with us.

504
37:42.991 --> 37:45.160
So I thought it would be anything but.

505
37:45.243 --> 37:46.661
[Je-hyeon] What are you talking about?

506
37:47.496 --> 37:48.914
Huh? What do you mean?

507
37:48.997 --> 37:52.125
What… What you just said. Who shared that information?

508
37:54.169 --> 37:56.338
Uh… well… [sighs]

509
37:56.421 --> 37:58.256
Did someone warn you about the cheongju?

510
37:58.882 --> 37:59.883
Well?

511
38:00.717 --> 38:01.802
Did they, or not?

512
38:03.261 --> 38:06.223
Ah… [sighs]

513
38:07.140 --> 38:08.683
Well, the truth is,

514
38:08.767 --> 38:11.019
this morning, Joshua and I were walking through…

515
38:11.103 --> 38:13.688
we were walking through the training center lobby

516
38:14.231 --> 38:16.066
when Chris called us over.

517
38:16.691 --> 38:19.361
He said he thought cheongju would be the liquor for this challenge.

518
38:20.737 --> 38:24.074
Then he started telling us how his team had figured it out.

519
38:24.157 --> 38:27.619
He talked about the aging period and how it's classified as yakju

520
38:27.702 --> 38:30.080
and not cheongju under the Liquor Tax Act

521
38:30.163 --> 38:31.957
and, well, things like that, you know?

522
38:32.040 --> 38:33.041
[sous-chef] And…

523
38:33.792 --> 38:36.086
why didn't you tell us any of that, Chef?

524
38:36.169 --> 38:38.630
Oh, I didn't believe it.

525
38:38.713 --> 38:41.508
I just assumed Chris had made the whole thing up to screw with us.

526
38:42.425 --> 38:45.345
[Je-hyeon sighs]

527
38:45.428 --> 38:48.098
Guys. Just think about it, huh?

528
38:48.181 --> 38:49.891
We're in a cutthroat competition.

529
38:49.975 --> 38:52.227
Who would just give away something they worked so hard

530
38:52.310 --> 38:53.812
to figure out by themselves?

531
38:53.895 --> 38:56.606
I figured it had to be a bluff, and that's it.

532
38:56.690 --> 38:59.484
Sure. Yeah.

533
38:59.568 --> 39:02.070
Come on, don't gang up on me now.

534
39:02.737 --> 39:05.073
I'm losing my mind here too.

535
39:05.574 --> 39:08.535
How was I supposed to know it really would be cheongju? Huh?

536
39:09.119 --> 39:10.453
Is that seriously your excuse?

537
39:11.329 --> 39:14.749
Chris was trying to help us by sharing information, and you blew it off?

538
39:14.833 --> 39:17.669
All three teams agreed to share our clues with each other.

539
39:17.752 --> 39:21.840
So of course Chris K. was trying to tell us what he knew about the dish.

540
39:21.923 --> 39:23.884
Have you been lied to your whole life?

541
39:26.011 --> 39:27.596
Why do you always have to be so cynical?

542
39:29.723 --> 39:31.099
-What? -I mean,

543
39:31.808 --> 39:33.185
if something like that happened,

544
39:33.268 --> 39:36.313
the least you could've done was tell me and your team about it.

545
39:36.396 --> 39:38.732
Isn't that basic courtesy to your team?

546
39:38.815 --> 39:41.151
Mr. Yang Ja-ryong, don't you have manners?

547
39:41.234 --> 39:42.903
Hey, now, "Park-san."

548
39:43.695 --> 39:47.699
"Park-san," watch your tongue now. Are you saying I did it on purpose?

549
39:48.283 --> 39:49.993
I thought I had already told you.

550
39:50.660 --> 39:54.247
I am not Japanese. I am Korean. I am Korean!

551
39:55.415 --> 39:57.167
[breathing heavily]

552
40:00.629 --> 40:02.797
This competition might be nothing more

553
40:02.881 --> 40:06.426
than a fun little cooking game to you, but it is not to me.

554
40:06.509 --> 40:08.178
I made a promise to my daughter.

555
40:08.261 --> 40:10.805
I'm here putting my name on the line for my daughter,

556
40:10.889 --> 40:12.140
who could die any day now.

557
40:12.224 --> 40:13.892
Do you have any idea what she's been through?

558
40:15.810 --> 40:16.811
Mr. Yang.

559
40:17.979 --> 40:19.231
Take this seriously, will you?

560
40:19.314 --> 40:21.524
Have you given it your all even once since you got here?

561
40:21.608 --> 40:24.110
You've just been phoning it in and fooling around.

562
40:24.194 --> 40:26.696
Aren't you even ashamed to face your sous-chef?

563
40:26.780 --> 40:27.781
-Hey. -[kicks metal]

564
40:28.740 --> 40:32.369
-Park Je-hyeon, you punk, I swear to you… -What?

565
40:32.452 --> 40:36.248
Oh, so you are ashamed to face him? I guess you do have some shame. Geez.

566
40:36.331 --> 40:38.041
Shut your damn mouth, or I'll…

567
40:38.708 --> 40:40.543
shut it for you, you hear?

568
40:40.627 --> 40:43.046
Listen to the way you are talking to me.

569
40:43.129 --> 40:46.174
Crude to the very end. Ugh, you're vulgar through and through.

570
40:46.925 --> 40:48.718
Hey, damn it.

571
40:49.928 --> 40:51.179
-Chef? Chef, chef. -I'm not taking this seriously?

572
40:51.263 --> 40:52.555
Your daughter's all that matters?

573
40:52.639 --> 40:54.432
-You're worried about your daughter, huh? -Whoa, Chef.

574
40:54.516 --> 40:55.892
But if I don't win this thing, I'm the one

575
40:55.976 --> 40:57.435
who's going to end up with a hole in my gut.

576
40:57.519 --> 40:59.062
-How do you like that? -Hold on, Chef.

577
40:59.145 --> 41:01.564
I'm just as serious as you are! You hear me, you jerk?

578
41:01.648 --> 41:03.608
-Let go of me, damn it! -Chef, please!

579
41:06.361 --> 41:08.488
-[all grunting] -Chef, Chef!

580
41:08.571 --> 41:10.824
-Let go! I said, let go! -Chef, please.

581
41:10.907 --> 41:13.285
[groaning] You little…

582
41:15.954 --> 41:18.456
-Please, stop! -Stop him, don't let him bite.

583
41:18.540 --> 41:21.293
-[gasps] Chef! The light! The light! -What light?

584
41:22.002 --> 41:23.586
Chef, this way.

585
41:50.280 --> 41:54.200
THE JUDGE NOTED THE ICY TENSION BETWEEN YOU

586
41:54.284 --> 41:55.869
[laughing] Come on! What are you talking about?

587
41:55.952 --> 41:57.037
PARK JE-HYEON OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

588
41:57.120 --> 41:58.371
YANG JA-RYONG HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

589
41:58.455 --> 42:01.082
Nah, it wasn't like that at all. Come on.

590
42:01.166 --> 42:05.879
You have no idea how good our… teamwork is. [chuckling]

591
42:05.962 --> 42:08.506
When you get into a heated discussion about food,

592
42:08.590 --> 42:10.050
voices can get a little loud.

593
42:10.133 --> 42:11.593
That's an industry standard.

594
42:11.676 --> 42:15.805
Goodness. You got it all wrong. [laughs]

595
42:16.431 --> 42:20.310
Well, uh, what are these walls made of? Plywood? Huh?

596
42:24.147 --> 42:26.483
They're concrete? Are you sure?

597
42:27.650 --> 42:31.946
Oh. They're made of concrete. Wow. [laughs]

598
42:32.030 --> 42:36.868
Well, anyway, we're Team Dragon-Sak. Please cheer for our team!

599
42:36.951 --> 42:39.996
We're… good!

600
42:41.790 --> 42:44.167
WE HEARD YOU EXPERIENCED DIFFICULTY

601
42:44.250 --> 42:46.836
PAIRING CHEONGJU WITH TTEOKBOKKI SAUCE

602
42:47.337 --> 42:49.089
Yes. Yes. That's true.

603
42:50.131 --> 42:52.759
It was difficult to balance the heat of gochujang

604
42:52.842 --> 42:55.345
with the clean, delicate character of cheongju.

605
42:55.428 --> 43:00.475
So I… I suggested adding an outer layer to make it richer and stronger,

606
43:00.558 --> 43:02.227
like the sugar coating on a pill.

607
43:02.310 --> 43:04.938
We made egg-shaped rice cakes

608
43:05.021 --> 43:07.232
filled with frozen tteokbokki sauce

609
43:07.315 --> 43:10.318
and chachang sauce, or curry sauce on it.

610
43:10.402 --> 43:12.612
[Ja-ryong] Finally, we coated them in semolina

611
43:12.695 --> 43:15.323
and fried them to create an eggshell-like crust.

612
43:15.407 --> 43:18.785
Then all we had to do was fry it so it'd be crispy on the outside

613
43:18.868 --> 43:20.036
and soft on the inside.

614
43:23.581 --> 43:27.836
We wanted to make something that would taste good but also be fun to eat,

615
43:27.919 --> 43:29.671
like peeling a boiled egg.

616
43:39.639 --> 43:41.516
[melancholy music playing]

617
44:12.505 --> 44:13.506
[camera operator] Ms. Seon.

618
44:14.591 --> 44:16.718
Choi Song-yi just went into Lab Two.

619
44:20.346 --> 44:22.599
Looks like she's going to try to do this on her own.

620
44:42.076 --> 44:43.953
We will put Lab 2 on air shortly.

621
44:44.621 --> 44:46.039
Be on standby to cut.

622
45:19.822 --> 45:22.242
[beeping]

623
45:28.873 --> 45:29.874
Hey.

624
45:32.168 --> 45:33.586
What are you doing?

625
45:36.256 --> 45:38.341
[inhales sharply] I asked what you're doing.

626
45:39.259 --> 45:41.302
Our second dish,

627
45:41.386 --> 45:43.221
infused with cheonghyang.

628
45:43.304 --> 45:45.306
What on earth are you…

629
45:46.140 --> 45:47.392
I'll be making it.

630
45:48.226 --> 45:49.602
Our team's second dish.

631
45:49.686 --> 45:50.687
[scoffs]

632
45:51.771 --> 45:54.691
What about Chef Chris? Where is he? When is he coming back?

633
46:18.464 --> 46:20.091
The knife.

634
46:21.676 --> 46:22.677
Put it down.

635
46:28.933 --> 46:31.185
It's okay. Put it down now.

636
46:32.770 --> 46:33.771
Ms. Choi.

637
46:45.116 --> 46:46.284
Don't worry.

638
46:48.494 --> 46:50.330
We'll replace the second dish

639
46:50.830 --> 46:52.457
with something else, it's okay.

640
46:53.207 --> 46:54.208
From now on,

641
46:55.835 --> 46:57.211
I'll need your help,

642
46:57.795 --> 46:58.796
Ms. Choi.

643
47:14.854 --> 47:16.105
[Han] Remember this.

644
47:17.148 --> 47:20.193
Cooking isn't about technique.

645
47:21.778 --> 47:23.821
It's about memory and imagination.

646
47:25.406 --> 47:27.784
[gentle music playing]

647
47:29.243 --> 47:30.495
The owner of Famiglia

648
47:31.162 --> 47:33.164
is you, Choi Song-yi.

649
47:34.332 --> 47:36.209
The person who should lead Famiglia

650
47:36.292 --> 47:37.919
is you, Choi Song-yi.

651
47:39.754 --> 47:41.089
Your father.

652
47:42.840 --> 47:44.884
He was killed by Pyeongyeon.

653
47:44.967 --> 47:47.095
We have to beat them.

654
47:47.804 --> 47:48.805
Together.

655
47:49.764 --> 47:51.391
[sighs] Listen, Song,

656
47:51.933 --> 47:53.810
you have instincts and sensitivity

657
47:54.769 --> 47:56.020
that no one else has.

658
47:56.104 --> 47:57.105
I know you do.

659
47:58.106 --> 48:01.275
I know you'll do this better than anyone.

660
48:29.053 --> 48:30.596
[sighs] Come on.

661
48:37.562 --> 48:38.563
Ms. Choi.

662
48:39.897 --> 48:42.734
We'll finely mince and mix poultry and game birds.

663
48:42.817 --> 48:45.278
Chicken, pheasant and quail as well.

664
48:45.361 --> 48:47.447
We'll wrap the meat in lotus leaves and smoke it,

665
48:47.530 --> 48:49.449
then fry them in gangjeong style pieces.

666
48:49.532 --> 48:51.951
We'll smoke it with dried lotus root.

667
48:52.577 --> 48:55.455
These maplewood chips are soaked in Jehyangju.

668
48:55.538 --> 48:58.833
Once they've absorbed the aroma of Jehyangju, we'll dry them

669
48:58.916 --> 49:00.460
and use them in a smoking gun.

670
49:00.543 --> 49:03.629
The key to this dish is to evoke memories.

671
49:03.713 --> 49:08.050
We're going to focus on smell, the most primal out of all the human senses.

672
49:08.134 --> 49:09.135
Cheonghyang…

673
49:13.806 --> 49:14.640
What's that?

674
49:14.724 --> 49:17.185
Water from Mount Gyesul, for the Jehyangju.

675
49:17.268 --> 49:21.189
It's been cold-infused with wildflowers from Mount Gyesul for an entire day.

676
49:21.272 --> 49:26.110
We'll use both the infused water and the wildflowers themselves.

677
49:39.207 --> 49:40.458
Let's do it.

678
49:40.541 --> 49:41.542
Yes, Chef.

679
50:31.509 --> 50:33.719
[water running gently]

680
50:36.639 --> 50:37.640
Dad.

681
50:39.725 --> 50:40.726
Hey, Dad.

682
50:42.019 --> 50:43.437
What are you thinking about?

683
50:51.612 --> 50:52.780
[sighs]

684
50:53.531 --> 50:54.949
I'm thinking about your mom.

685
50:59.829 --> 51:01.080
Mom?

686
51:01.789 --> 51:02.790
Yeah.

687
51:04.083 --> 51:05.543
Do those flowers…

688
51:09.005 --> 51:10.214
remind you of Mom?

689
51:20.766 --> 51:22.268
[Seung-hyo] They're beautiful.

690
51:26.981 --> 51:28.399
They're beautiful…

691
51:30.443 --> 51:31.652
and sad.

692
51:39.243 --> 51:40.953
Just like your mom was.

693
51:59.722 --> 52:02.141
[emotional music playing]

694
52:10.983 --> 52:12.860
[door opening]

695
52:35.758 --> 52:37.343
[sighs]

696
52:51.649 --> 52:52.942
[sighs deeply]

697
52:59.782 --> 53:02.076
What's the matter? What happened, Han?

698
53:04.078 --> 53:05.371
Oh, come on.

699
53:07.415 --> 53:10.292
Hey, don't just stand there. Let's put it in another container.

700
53:23.848 --> 53:25.224
Can't we just throw it out?

701
53:26.392 --> 53:27.893
It smells rotten.

702
53:27.977 --> 53:30.229
Why would you throw this away? It's fine.

703
53:30.312 --> 53:34.275
We'll have to throw out the bottom half, but we can still eat the top half.

704
53:34.358 --> 53:36.527
Do you know how precious this is?

705
53:37.111 --> 53:38.696
I practically had to beg the landlord

706
53:38.779 --> 53:41.240
to let me keep it buried in the yard for three whole months.

707
53:41.323 --> 53:44.744
Without this, I might have to go to LA to get more.

708
53:48.122 --> 53:51.959
There's no Koreatown here, so Korean ingredients are really hard to come by.

709
53:52.043 --> 53:53.919
Be careful next time.

710
54:00.009 --> 54:01.427
Exactly.

711
54:02.053 --> 54:04.096
Can't we stop moving around so much?

712
54:06.932 --> 54:08.934
Can't we stay in one place, Dad?

713
54:23.991 --> 54:25.534
[gentle music playing]

714
54:27.703 --> 54:28.704
Well, Han.

715
54:29.789 --> 54:30.873
It's been tough, huh?

716
54:32.083 --> 54:34.919
[sighs] Just a little longer.

717
54:35.002 --> 54:36.212
Hang in there, please.

718
54:36.295 --> 54:37.713
How much longer?

719
54:40.424 --> 54:42.551
How long do we have to keep running like this?

720
54:43.302 --> 54:46.347
How long do we have to keep running like we've done something wrong?

721
54:48.390 --> 54:49.767
[sighs]

722
54:52.144 --> 54:53.979
Will Mom kill us if she finds us?

723
54:55.189 --> 54:57.691
Is she trying to catch us so she can kill us?

724
54:57.775 --> 55:00.152
-Stop it! -Does she want to kill us to make sure

725
55:00.236 --> 55:02.446
I never see you or live with you again, huh?

726
55:14.542 --> 55:16.460
[crying quietly]

727
55:17.128 --> 55:18.379
[groans]

728
55:18.462 --> 55:19.797
I'm sorry, Han.

729
55:28.305 --> 55:29.348
Han.

730
55:38.816 --> 55:39.900
Han.

731
55:52.538 --> 55:54.623
[pop ballad playing]

732
56:32.036 --> 56:33.954
WAIVER OF PARENTAL RIGHTS

733
56:35.539 --> 56:37.833
-[pounding on door] -Dad!

734
56:38.792 --> 56:40.669
Dad, please!

735
56:40.753 --> 56:41.921
[young Han crying]

736
56:45.883 --> 56:47.760
[ballad continues playing]

737
57:00.064 --> 57:02.983
SCHOOL NURSE'S OFFICE

738
57:47.736 --> 57:49.154
What happened?

739
57:59.039 --> 58:00.040
What's up?

740
58:01.333 --> 58:03.294
[utensil clatters on ground]

741
58:05.212 --> 58:09.591
What now? What's going on? There's always something, geez!

742
58:09.675 --> 58:10.759
I think the power went out.

743
58:10.843 --> 58:11.927
What?

744
58:13.262 --> 58:15.723
A blackout? [sighs]

745
58:24.315 --> 58:26.650
[gasping]

746
58:27.401 --> 58:29.403
They say the west wing circuit overloaded.

747
58:29.486 --> 58:31.613
-What do we do? -[sighs shakily]

748
58:34.325 --> 58:36.118
[audience murmuring]

749
58:55.054 --> 58:56.638
Looks like the power's gone out.

750
59:05.647 --> 59:07.483
[breathing shakily]

751
59:14.615 --> 59:15.616
Han, wait.

752
59:23.082 --> 59:25.376
Find the Jehyangju footage we had on standby earlier.

753
59:25.459 --> 59:26.543
-On it! -Hurry!

754
59:27.628 --> 59:29.380
Captain Cook, we've got a problem over here.

755
59:29.463 --> 59:31.673
I'll find the Jehyangju footage and send it over.

756
59:31.757 --> 59:33.300
Buy us as much time as you can. Hurry.

757
59:34.802 --> 59:38.263
[dramatic music playing]

758
01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:24.059
[pop song playing]

759
01:00:36.613 --> 01:00:38.031
[Han] This time,

760
01:00:38.115 --> 01:00:40.742
we can't let things go her way.

761
01:00:40.826 --> 01:00:43.328
[Woo-jin] Twenty-seven minutes. Can you do it?

762
01:00:43.996 --> 01:00:48.625
[Captain Cook] And now, it's finally time to announce the final rankings.

763
01:00:48.709 --> 01:00:51.336
[Hyeon-ju] Must've been rough, laying low all this time.

764
01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:53.464
[reporters clamoring]

765
01:00:53.547 --> 01:00:54.715
What does this mean?

766
01:00:54.798 --> 01:00:56.842
[Song-yi] Pyeongyeon Group's chairwoman's…

767
01:00:56.925 --> 01:00:58.552
Was that the reason?

768
01:01:00.471 --> 01:01:01.638
For my father,

769
01:01:03.182 --> 01:01:04.933
who died in agony.
